{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541673-mtg-dusk-legion-conversion-concept","id":1541673,"title":"MTG Dusk Legion Conversion concept","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-09-06T20:24:41-07:00","id":1541673,"title":"MTG Dusk Legion Conversion concept","content":"I'm proposing a character concept for interplay in WOD. This is a conversion of the MTA vampire breed of the plane of Ixalon: The Dusk Legion.<br /> <br /> Essentially, hyper religious and disciplined conquistador vampires.<br /> <br /> The DL has several characteristics that set them apart from other breeds, and THEY ARE NOT TRUE VAMPIRES strictly speaking. They are more akin to the tremere before they became a true bloodline.<br /> <br /> -can walk in sunlight: <br /> this is the first issue that will come up gameplaywise. This is likely a consequence of their extreme faith (or other things), but has its trade off. DL don't have blood art like superpowers; instead relying on innate vampire strength and speed combined with discipline and martial prowess. Like a thin blood but w/ normal vamp strength. <br /> <br /> -origin: <br /> They were created by a desperate religious knight who was given an immortality ritual by an aztec-esque bat-demon god. ALL DL are create via a blood ritual similar to the communal blood-bond of the Sabat use. DL cannot be made except via ritual. They are essentially religious artificial vampires.<br /> <br /> - beliefs:<br /> the church misinterpreted the ritual as a form of holy martyrdom given by god. They view blood as a holy material to be respected, and even go on &quot;blood fasting&quot;, giving them high resistance to the urge to feed. They are one of the only vampire types that can very easily convince themselves they are the &quot;good guys&quot;<br /> <br /> Ranks: <br /> they are essentially a religious order of paladins .... but vampires. They come in 2 flavors: conquistador-esque knight, or priest/cleric. <br /> <br /> TLDR:<br /> Artificial vampires created by a bat demon via a ritual he made to prank Christians into believing vampires are living saints.<br /> <br /> There are definitely some &quot;Alexander Anderson&quot; stuff that could be done with this concept. It could also do well in a human focused crossplay game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27527-devilturnip","name":"Devilturnip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-06T23:07:32-07:00","id":1541678,"title":"","content":"Maybe you should frame them not as VtM vampires, but as a particular type of ghoul able to feed on human blood and able to take Potence, Fortitude and Celerity up to 5 dots, but no other discipline.<br /> Or a different creature altogether with a single stat akin to VtR Blood Potency. They have no disciplines, but their Blood Potency is added automatically to all physical rolls, and can become automatic successes for a roll by spending 1 blood point.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200bSince the origin story is connected to something akin to the Aztecs, plus a bat-demon, you can easily justify them as a Drowned Legacy native to South or Central America who can <i>pass for</i> vampires despite not actually being vampires.<br /> <br /> There is a bat spirit (Bat) from that area in the lore linked to werewolves and fera, particularly the Camazotz, who has fallen to the Wyrm. He used to teach the Camazotz blood rituals, and they used to be made through a ritual as well. You could adapt him as your bat-demon.<br /> Alternatively there's at least two potential demon/vampires in VtM Aztec lore: Tezcatlipoca and Huitzilopoctli. Reading up on them might give you some idea on how they may be linked to the origin of your Dusk Legion. Notice that Huitzilopoctli may be linked - due to the Baali - to the creation of one of the first revenant families, the D'habi. You could posit that the Dusk Legion is a later experiment of his along those lines, especially if you frame them as some sort of peculiar ghoul.<br /> The use of a demon/vampire NPC helps also because WoD demons feed on faith, so it makes sense that one might opt to produce an extra religious group.<br /> <br /> Option 3: a bloodline with four in-clan disciplines (I think the Gargoyles also had four in Revised), namely the three physical ones plus a fourth giving resistance or immunity to sunlight, but their clan weakness is that they cannot learn any other discipline, period, except whatever you need to give them for their blood rituals (but you could handle that better with a background analogous to the Sabbat Ritae if they are not very powerful).<br /> <br /> Hope this helps.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":16965,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","user":"Hermeticus","date":". 09-07-2024, 02:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-21T08:47:41-07:00","id":1542481,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1541673-mtg-dusk-legion-conversion-concept?p=1541678#post1541678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe you should frame them not as VtM vampires, but as a particular type of ghoul able to feed on human blood and able to take Potence, Fortitude and Celerity up to 5 dots, but no other discipline.<br /> Or a different creature altogether with a single stat akin to VtR Blood Potency. They have no disciplines, but their Blood Potency is added automatically to all physical rolls, and can become automatic successes for a roll by spending 1 blood point.<br /> \u200b\u200bSince the origin story is connected to something akin to the Aztecs, plus a bat-demon, you can easily justify them as a Drowned Legacy native to South or Central America who can <i>pass for</i> vampires despite not actually being vampires.<br /> <br /> There is a bat spirit (Bat) from that area in the lore linked to werewolves and fera, particularly the Camazotz, who has fallen to the Wyrm. He used to teach the Camazotz blood rituals, and they used to be made through a ritual as well. You could adapt him as your bat-demon.<br /> Alternatively there's at least two potential demon/vampires in VtM Aztec lore: Tezcatlipoca and Huitzilopoctli. Reading up on them might give you some idea on how they may be linked to the origin of your Dusk Legion. Notice that Huitzilopoctli may be linked - due to the Baali - to the creation of one of the first revenant families, the D'habi. You could posit that the Dusk Legion is a later experiment of his along those lines, especially if you frame them as some sort of peculiar ghoul.<br /> The use of a demon/vampire NPC helps also because WoD demons feed on faith, so it makes sense that one might opt to produce an extra religious group.<br /> <br /> Option 3: a bloodline with four in-clan disciplines (I think the Gargoyles also had four in Revised), namely the three physical ones plus a fourth giving resistance or immunity to sunlight, but their clan weakness is that they cannot learn any other discipline, period, except whatever you need to give them for their blood rituals (but you could handle that better with a background analogous to the Sabbat Ritae if they are not very powerful).<br /> <br /> Hope this helps.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for your help, I like ALL of these ideas.<br /> <br /> I had contemplated adapting kuei-jin mechanics, but combining these concepts (roughly) might give me the sweets spot I'm looking for.<br /> <br /> Especially the Bali bat demon potentially being a corrupted Camazotz spirit. The mtg inspiration is the bat-demon-god Aclazotz, who desires blood sacrifices. He tried to make vampires both sacrifice too him, AND emulate him by taking sacrifices at their will ( ie: sabbat style reckless feeding)<br /> <br /> This might better balance my character concept:<br /> They're not true vampires, but INCOMPLETE bat demons. Spawned by a spiteful bat demon that tricked religious conquistadors into hunting the Mokole...... for some reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27527-devilturnip","name":"Devilturnip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}